From: pmOS Q706F fixes
Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: add ov8856 and ov13b10 sensor helpers and properties

Lenovo TB-Q706F has an OV8856 front camera and an OV13B10 rear camera
on qcom-camss. With libcamera 0.7.1 the simple pipeline and soft ISP
work, but they warn that there is no sensor helper and no static
properties for either sensor, so AGC has no gain model and the sensor
delays are guessed.

Sources for the values:
- Analogue gain: both Linux drivers write V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN as is
  to 0x3508/0x3509. ov8856 has a minimum of 128 and ov13b10 a range
  of 0x80-0x7c0 with 0x80 as default, so 128 is 1x. This is the same
  1/128 step as the existing ov8858 and ov13858 helpers. Not checked
  against a datasheet.
- Black level: estimated, 64 at 10 bits (the usual OmniVision value).
  Raw captures on the tablet are consistent with it (low percentiles
  67-71 for ov8856 and 63-64 for ov13b10); these were scenes, not dark
  frames.
- Unit cell size: 1.12 um for both, from the OmniVision product briefs.
- Test patterns: from the driver menus; only "off" and the first colour
  bar map to libcamera modes.
- Sensor delays: estimated (2 frames each, as for ov64a40); not
  measured.

Based on Fedora 44 libcamera-0.7.1-1 (v0.7.1 + 0001-disable-rpi-pisp).

---
diff --git a/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp b/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp
index e3e3e53..cccc4e9 100644
--- a/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp
@@ -753,6 +753,29 @@ public:
 };
 REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("ov64a40", CameraSensorHelperOv64a40)
 
+class CameraSensorHelperOv8856 : public CameraSensorHelper
+{
+public:
+	CameraSensorHelperOv8856()
+	{
+		/*
+		 * Black level: estimated, not from the datasheet. 64 at 10 bits
+		 * is the usual OmniVision default; on a Lenovo TB-Q706F raw
+		 * capture the 0.01-1 % percentiles were 67-71 (scene, not a
+		 * dark frame), which is consistent with it.
+		 */
+		blackLevel_ = 4096;
+		/*
+		 * The Linux ov8856 driver writes V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN as is to
+		 * 0x3508/0x3509 with a minimum of 128, so 128 is 1x. This is
+		 * the same 1/128 step as the ov8858 and ov13858 helpers. Not
+		 * checked against the datasheet.
+		 */
+		gain_ = AnalogueGainLinear{ 1, 0, 0, 128 };
+	}
+};
+REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("ov8856", CameraSensorHelperOv8856)
+
 class CameraSensorHelperOv8858 : public CameraSensorHelper
 {
 public:
@@ -789,6 +812,29 @@ public:
 };
 REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("ov13858", CameraSensorHelperOv13858)
 
+class CameraSensorHelperOv13b10 : public CameraSensorHelper
+{
+public:
+	CameraSensorHelperOv13b10()
+	{
+		/*
+		 * Black level: estimated, not from the datasheet. 64 at 10 bits
+		 * is the usual OmniVision default; on a Lenovo TB-Q706F raw
+		 * capture the 0.1-1 % percentiles were 63-64 (scene, not a
+		 * dark frame), which is consistent with it.
+		 */
+		blackLevel_ = 4096;
+		/*
+		 * The Linux ov13b10 driver writes V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN as is
+		 * to 0x3508/0x3509, range 0x80-0x7c0 with 0x80 as default, so
+		 * 0x80 is 1x and 0x7c0 is 15.5x. Same 1/128 step as ov13858.
+		 * Not checked against the datasheet.
+		 */
+		gain_ = AnalogueGainLinear{ 1, 0, 0, 128 };
+	}
+};
+REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("ov13b10", CameraSensorHelperOv13b10)
+
 class CameraSensorHelperVd55g1 : public CameraSensorHelper
 {
 public:
